  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>The butter catfish, Ompok bimaculatus, is a widespread silurid catfish recorded from Afghanistan and Pakistan east to China and Indonesia. Also known as the two spot glass catfish, it very likely contains a number of separate species, given the clear morphological differences between populations. It grows to 45 cm, needs a very large tank and will eat any smaller fish it can fit in its mouth.</p><ul><li>Difficulty: Moderate. Rated moderate because it needs a very large tank.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>A widespread species having been recorded in Afghanistan, India, Pakistan, Nepal, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, China, Myanmar, Thailand, Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam and Indonesia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>In what would seem a direct correlation with its ample distribution, this fish is found in a variety of biotopes, from slow-moving, turbid streams, to canals and flowing rivers. The fish tend to migrate to flooded areas during the wet season.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 45 cm SL.</p><p>Aquarium base: 180 × 60 cm.</p><p>Volume: ~648 litres (180 × 60 × 60 cm).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>This species requires a lot of open swimming space, along with some suitably-sized hiding places. Lengths of thick plastic piping should be adequate for this, as it is doubtful that any aquatic plants could withstand the movements of a fish this size. Filtration should be powerful and efficient, as this fish is a messy eater and produces a lot of waste. It is also sensitive to deteriorating water conditions and regular water changes must be strictly observed. Some degree of current is also preferred.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 20–26°C</li><li>pH: 6.0–8.0</li><li>Hardness: 4–28 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Relatively unfussy and will take most live, frozen and dried foods. Offer larger specimens earthworms, pieces of prawn and mussel and dried pellets.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Fairly peaceful and shy for its size, although it will eat any smaller fish it can fit in its mouth. It has a mouth full of razor sharp teeth to reinforce this point. Good tankmates include medium to large sized cyprinids, characins, similarly-sized Asian or South American catfish and larger cichlids.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Males have a serrated edge to the pectoral spine, which females lack. Mature females are thicker-bodied than males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>Thus far only achieved through the use of hormone injections in captivity.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/ompok-bimaculatus",
      "html": "<p>This infrequently seen (in the trade) species is also known as the two spot glass catfish, for fairly obvious reasons. It is more than likely that a number of separate species are currently considered within this one, as there are clear morphological differences between populations. Pending a taxonomic revision, these currently all remain classified as O. bimaculatus. This would also seem to explain the apparent toleration of the fish to wildly different water chemistry.</p><p>Xanthic and albino forms are also occasionally available in the hobby, although these seem to be confined to the Southeast Asian 'species'. Taxonomy aside, it's considered a fine food fish in many of its native countries in both fresh and smoked forms.</p>"
    }
  ],
